course: dictionary
lesson: 9329
title: Spiky: sharp points and prickly moods
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Spiky means having many sharp points sticking out. A cactus is spiky, and so is a hedgehog. «A sweet chestnut has long toothed leaves» shows that the leaves have points too, so you can see what spiky means without looking it up.
explain: Spiky is also used for hair that is stiff and sticks up in points. Spiky can describe a mood, too. Informally, a spiky person is easily annoyed and unfriendly. If someone gives a spiky reply, they are probably cross, not friendly.
explain: Check the context. «A spiky case usually holds one conker» is about a prickly case. «Blakiston's fish owl grabs salmon with rough, spiky soles» is about the bottom of the owl's feet, which have sharp points that help it hold on to slippery fish.
fact: Spiky means having many sharp points.
fact: Spiky hair is stiff and sticks up in points.
fact: «Spiky» can also describe a person who is easily annoyed and unfriendly.
fact: «A sweet chestnut's case is much spikier than a horse chestnut's.» This means its case has more, or sharper, points.
fact: Plants and animals such as cacti, hedgehogs and sea urchins can use spikes to protect themselves.
exercise: «Its case is much spikier than a horse chestnut's.» What does spikier mean here? | Shinier and brighter | *Having more sharp points | Smoother and softer | Heavier and rounder
exercise: Lily says spiky hair is soft and floppy. What is the right meaning of spiky hair? | Hair that is long and smooth | Hair that is wet and flat | *Hair that is stiff and sticks up in points | Hair that is curly and soft
exercise: «She gave a spiky reply and slammed the door.» What does spiky suggest about her? | Happy and kind | Very tired and sleepy | *Easily annoyed and unfriendly
test: «Electron microscopes show the spiky shapes of pollen grains.» What does spiky describe here? | Very large, heavy objects | Soft, round shapes | Bright, glowing colours | *Shapes with many sharp points
test: Why might a sea urchin be spiky? | *To protect itself with sharp points | To make it float faster | To keep itself safe from being eaten | To help it sing
exam: «A spiky ball» is a ball covered in sharp points; which of these is the best meaning? | A ball that is very light and glows | A ball that bounces very high | *A ball covered in sharp points that sticks out | A ball that is soft and squashy
